in parentheses and place the degree symbol outside, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{360}{18}\\right)}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac9b94a-deba-5ead-bd09-5b35c74bd0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{160}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619180} {:answer/id #uuid "483458d6-3208-5e13-92e8-8f651daaf392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619278} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5b972b-3fe3-5cca-884b-a3aa217ebc77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619428} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7848b8-9aab-51d4-805f-ffe42669d315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{200}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619505} {:answer/id #uuid "dc453c51-7c59-526c-a95b-34cb51b176d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e5b972b-3fe3-5cca-884b-a3aa217ebc77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619428} :answer-field/id #uuid "c55d066b-4f42-5c6b-b5b5-ef9b8fd50c7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca45fa12-c8d2-5bbc-86dc-a03641b889be" :question/math-academy-id "q-150716" :question/problem "![](images/25/25fefa52a60e9cecfd5c688f459737e33d5d9eddce55d7abeac70b173af9508d.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $∠AOB$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a full turn. What is the degree measure of the angle?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a full turn has a measure of ${360}^{∘}.$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $∠AOB$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\times {360}^{∘} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times {360}^{∘}}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{{360}^{∘}}{2} & = \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{360}{2}\\right)}^{∘} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the value of $360÷2$ using a box model:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"60\">$100$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"60\">$80$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"15\" width=\"15\">\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\">\n$2$\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"60\">\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30\" width=\"60\">\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\boxed{\\text{0}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"85\" width=\"60\">\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nAccording to the box model,\n\n$$\n360÷2=100+80=180.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $∠AOB$ is ${180}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c63c2f0-b42a-526c-96c4-d2a3518af31e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c63c2f0-b42a-526c-96c4-d2a3518af31e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619425} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f3c9600-f416-5c1d-84ac-31592b4239ac" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619631} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d521ef4f-322d-51e4-8c5e-f56955728342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d521ef4f-322d-51e4-8c5e-f56955728342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619521} :answer-field/id #uuid "889b893e-0423-5738-9c7c-11337ded5673" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e65fffe3-652d-5bfb-949d-8d2dbec0be26" :question/math-academy-id "q-173716" :question/problem "$3p+(-2)(p+q)=${{field-1}}\n$p\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$q$" :question/worked-solution "First, we distribute the $(-2)$ over each of the terms in parentheses.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p+(-2)(p+q) & = \\\\ 3p+(-2)\\cdot p+(-2)\\cdot q & = \\\\ 3p+(-2p)+(-2q) & = \\\\ 3p-2p-2q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-2p-2q & = \\\\ (3p-2p)-2q & = \\\\ 1\\,p-2\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186619811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd487946-6842-5fcd-a37f-3594c54b4b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd487946-6842-5fcd-a37f-3594c54b4b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619478} :answer-field/id #uuid "e179556a-4a02-5acf-8e10-7825f54a865a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619697} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "965d8381-ba92-5223-95ac-d94560271a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "965d8381-ba92-5223-95ac-d94560271a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619411} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0f5b51a-111a-5530-83d6-c4f166db1320" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186619703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3cbb6d7-f146-5015-aa81-391efc8ccb02" :question/math-academy-id "q-178185" :question/problem "$2p+(p+4q)\\cdot 6=${{field-1}}\n$p\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$q$" :question/worked-solution "First, we distribute the $6$ over each of the terms in parentheses.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p+(p+4q)\\cdot 6 & = \\\\ 2p+p\\cdot 6+4q\\cdot 6 & = \\\\ 2p+(6p)+(24q) & = \\\\ 2p+6p+24q & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p+6p+24q & = \\\\ (2p+6p)+24q & = \\\\ 8\\,p+24\\,q\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186622212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a092c5-308a-54a6-a35b-0833008503a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b^{18}\\cdot c^{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186622211} {:answer/id #uuid "75cbf483-fc11-59a5-9e7e-0b6b15a9fe5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b^{17}\\cdot c^{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186622214} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cdbdcc-a2e9-5e30-98d6-8635d6fbbc98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b^{21}\\cdot c^{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186622215} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f415e1-45e9-5b76-91ec-ac4a7ccd23d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b^{16}\\cdot c^{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186622216} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b583b6-e5e4-5639-b56a-a0bbf273ecba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b^{22}\\cdot c^{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186622217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43a092c5-308a-54a6-a35b-0833008503a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b^{18}\\cdot c^{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186622211} :answer-field/id #uuid "73d44035-03e3-5cb5-abf7-df912522b039" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186622213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59695386-1734-5009-a901-d41cf37e1cfe" :question/problem "Use exponents to rewrite the product $c\\cdot b^{5}\\cdot c^{3}\\cdot b^{6}\\cdot c^{6}\\cdot c^{4}\\cdot b^{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here we have 2 variables, $b$ and $c$. We will start by grouping the bases separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot b^{5}\\cdot c^{3}\\cdot b^{6}\\cdot c^{6}\\cdot c^{4}\\cdot b^{7} &= \\\\ (b^{5}\\cdot b^{6}\\cdot b^{7})\\cdot (c^{1}\\cdot c^{3}\\cdot c^{6}\\cdot c^{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis way, we can apply the product rule to the bases independently. An unwritten exponent is 1:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(b^{5}\\cdot b^{6}\\cdot b^{7})\\cdot (c^{1}\\cdot c^{3}\\cdot c^{6}\\cdot c^{4}) &= \\\\ b^{(5+6+7)}\\cdot c^{(1+3+6+4)} &= \\\\ b^{18}\\cdot c^{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a5e98b9-84c6-57b3-961d-d52f1db741a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623189} {:answer/id #uuid "15aa8161-1925-526e-ac8d-b3985e84d9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623208} {:answer/id #uuid "c198db15-a924-5347-800d-f68da0bc276d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623435} {:answer/id #uuid "efcfec1a-cd5e-5ff2-bdbb-d866ee628e3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623504} {:answer/id #uuid "f931e94c-5196-5c70-a774-7f26f3329d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15aa8161-1925-526e-ac8d-b3985e84d9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623208} :answer-field/id #uuid "85a93f38-868b-5527-b7f3-de62613a92b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "077c96c6-1587-5c37-8919-f61c4d44042a" :question/math-academy-id "q-91833" :question/problem "In his last $4$ chess tournaments, Grandmaster Carlson achieved a mean of $3$ wins per tournament; the number of victories he obtained in each of those tournaments is given below:\n\n$$\n3,\\,5,\\,2,\\,2\n$$\n\n \n\nThe mean absolute deviation (MAD) of Grandmaster Dong (who competed in the same tournaments) is $0.7.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The MAD for Grandmaster Carlson's wins is $0.9.$\n\nII. Grandmaster Dong's MAD is smaller than Grandmaster Carlson's.\n\nIII. A comparison of the MADs suggests it's easier to predict the number of games Grandmaster Carlson will win in the next tournament." :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the mean absolute deviation for Carlson's wins.\n\nSince we are given the mean ($3$),  we subtract it from each number in the data set, find the absolute value of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3-3|+|5-3|+|2-3|+|2-3| & = \\\\ |0|+|2|+|-1|+|\\,-1\\,| & = \\\\ 0+2+1+1 & = \\\\ 4 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation, we divide the result ($4$) by the total number of data points ($4$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD}=\\frac{4}{4}=1\n$$\n\nSo, Carlson's wins had a mean absolute deviation of $1.$  \n\nLet's now examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $0.7<1$, Grandmaster Dong's MAD is smaller than Grandmaster Carlson's.\n\n- Statement III is false. Grandmaster Carlson's scores are more spread out, indicating more variability in the data than Grandmaster Dong's, suggesting that it's easier to predict how many games Grandmaster Dong will win in the next tournament.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24d4a254-dd00-58ea-9105-3411d970cd8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623224} {:answer/id #uuid "49703a4a-a206-5198-a136-302bef45c983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623274} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5c848e-3044-5a22-8813-b542b9f2e924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623313} {:answer/id #uuid "817b900c-2e9b-564f-9594-322f7217fc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623349} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff71e37-52e5-5284-a83d-25cf52c6bb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ff71e37-52e5-5284-a83d-25cf52c6bb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623365} :answer-field/id #uuid "85466a4b-7b61-5d36-8704-3d2c0fc1086c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "494a8aff-8f3e-593f-8d2b-29abec12553d" :question/math-academy-id "q-318856" :question/problem "A wellness app logged a user’s daily step counts (in steps) for four days. The counts are shown below:\n\n$$\n6600,\\,6800,\\,7400,\\,7200\n$$\n\nThe user’s mean step count was $7000$ steps.\n\nAnother user completed a similar four-day set with a mean absolute deviation (MAD) of $500$ steps. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The first user’s step counts have a mean absolute deviation of $500.$\n\nII. A comparison of the MADs suggests the second user’s step counts have a smaller spread than the first user’s.\n\nIII. A comparison of the MADs suggests the second user’s step counts have the same spread as the first user’s." :question/worked-solution "Let’s compute the mean absolute deviation for the first user’s step counts.\n\nSince we are given the mean ($7000$), we subtract it from each number in the data set, find the absolute value of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|6600-7000|+|6800-7000|+|7400-7000|+|7200-7000| & = \\\\ 400+200+400+200 & = \\\\ 1200 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above ($1200$) by the total number of data points ($4$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD}=\\frac{1200}{4}=300\n$$\n\nSo, the first user’s step counts have a mean absolute deviation of $300$ steps.\n\nLet's now examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n- Statements II and III are both false. Since $500>300$, the second user’s step counts have a larger spread, not smaller or the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "508efe4a-b2e3-5b54-8361-1602508a584a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623290} {:answer/id #uuid "7b25491a-2f7c-5114-a909-17eb3819d26b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623343} {:answer/id #uuid "81955058-9e8f-57c0-a69f-a9d8b6931380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623350} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cdfaae-6b88-5115-8b70-b5988a5a42db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623399} {:answer/id #uuid "ff18090b-ba6a-550b-9f19-fa0ccbe0ff63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81955058-9e8f-57c0-a69f-a9d8b6931380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623350} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d2cddf3-b689-5845-b88c-8256e6d3013a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "689331c3-b14e-5db0-a97b-c8ba755046c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-63344" :question/problem "Matt obtained a mean score of $87.5$ points on the last four math tests. His scores were as follows:\n\n$$\n85,\\,87,\\,88,\\,90\n$$\n\nRosie's scores on the same tests show a mean absolute deviation (MAD) of $1.5$ points.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The MAD of Matt's scores is $1.2$ points.\n\nII. Matt's MAD is smaller than Rosie's.\n\nIII. A comparison of the MADs suggests Rosie performed more consistently in the tests." :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the mean absolute deviation for Matt's scores.\n\nSince we are given the mean ($88$), we subtract it from each number in the data set, find the absolute value of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|85-87.5|+|87-87.5|+|88-87.5|+|90-87.5| & = \\\\ |-2.5|+|-0.5|+|\\,0.5\\,|+|\\,2.5\\,| & = \\\\ 2.5+0.5+0.5+2.5 & = \\\\ 6 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above ($6$) by the total number of data points ($4$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD}=\\frac{6}{4}=1.5\n$$\n\nSo, Matt's scores have a mean absolute deviation of $1.5$ points. \n\nLet's now examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matt's and Rosie's scores have the same MAD.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since Matt and Rosie have the same MAD, there is no suggestion that Rosie performed more consistently than Matt.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186623695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5099e6be-c684-5f6a-9b55-601232ab5d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623291} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e1d13c-c002-577a-b4b7-7ce82d29202c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623408} {:answer/id #uuid "b129839c-2dd5-5dc5-821d-0af6ba93366f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623410} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f572e1-6d0c-5afb-9e5d-8a499dae4566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623416} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4540c6-79f6-5ce4-99bb-26e153eae820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5099e6be-c684-5f6a-9b55-601232ab5d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623291} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b173d3c-81a3-5f29-bc09-3e5d7ee4ee03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e9fe1422-0674-5fab-ba54-ff869ef09844" :question/math-academy-id "q-91829" :question/problem "In their last $5$ games against the Braves, the Yankees had a mean of $4$ home runs per game. The number of home runs scored by the Yankees in each game is given below:\n\n$$\n5,\\,3,\\,3,\\,5,\\,4\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the Braves completed the games with a mean absolute deviation (MAD) of $0.7$ home runs. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The MAD of the Yankees' home runs was $0.8.$\n\nII. The Yankees' MAD is greater than the Braves'.\n\nIII. A comparison of the MADs suggests it's easier to predict how many home runs the Braves will score next time the two teams play." :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the mean absolute deviation for the Yankees.\n\nSince we are given Yankees' mean ($4$), we subtract it from each number in the data set, find the absolute value of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5-4|+|3-4|+|3-4|+|5-4|+|4-4| & = \\\\ |\\,1\\,|+|\\,-1\\,|+|\\,-1\\,|+|1|+|0| & = \\\\ 1+1+1+1+0 & = \\\\ 4 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above ($4$) by the total number of data points ($5$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD}=\\frac{4}{5}=0.8\n$$\n\nSo, the Yankees' have a mean absolute deviation of $0.8$ home runs. \n\nLet's now examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $0.8>0.7$, the Yankees' MAD is greater than the Braves'.\n\n- Statement III is true. The Yankees' scores are more spread out, indicating more variability in the data than the Braves', suggesting that it's easier to predict the number of home runs the Braves will score in the next game.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186624532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1ed513-6719-54f1-a9ae-0d8ed44fac75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3({b}^{2}+\\sqrt{b})}{b-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186623922} {:answer/id #uuid "4cde0b9c-d9f7-59b9-befd-b6ca702cd3c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(b+\\sqrt{b})}{b-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186624043} {:answer/id #uuid "5feb9e1b-fd74-5090-81f8-9d2fc06bd43a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1+\\sqrt{b})}{b}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186624089} {:answer/id #uuid "7750b11a-7aa2-549e-b87a-12dab969e0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3b-\\sqrt{b}}{b+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186624129} {:answer/id #uuid "8f96f5a4-d555-5452-8822-9d569c670c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3b-\\sqrt{b}}{b-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186624178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cde0b9c-d9f7-59b9-befd-b6ca702cd3c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(b+\\sqrt{b})}{b-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186624043} :answer-field/id #uuid "28cc23dd-a2ff-5ebc-86b3-beab250c790b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186624389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "616b0fc0-e080-5cdf-851e-d4d9e56703ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-34529" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{3b}{b-\\sqrt{b}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $b+\\sqrt{b}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $b+\\sqrt{b},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3b}{b-\\sqrt{b}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3b}{b-\\sqrt{b}}\\cdot \\frac{b+\\sqrt{b}}{b+\\sqrt{b}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3b(b+\\sqrt{b})}{(b-\\sqrt{b})(b+\\sqrt{b})} & = \\\\ \\frac{3b(b+\\sqrt{b})}{{b}^{2}-(\\sqrt{b})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3b(b+\\sqrt{b})}{{b}^{2}-b} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cancel{b}(b+\\sqrt{b})}{\\cancel{b}(b-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(b+\\sqrt{b})}{b-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
